{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":1,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1500108583,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDU6SXNzdWUyNDMxNjI5MDc=","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1642","origin":"github"},"title":"Why do we need to instruct GitPython to clean up?","message":"Upfront confession: I still do not believe that wrapt is at fault re the notorious segfault.\n\nPlease help me understand why this code is required in `GitRepo.__del__`:\n\n````\n        # Make sure to flush pending changes, especially close batch processes\n        # (internal `git cat-file --batch` by GitPython)\n        if hasattr(self, 'repo') and self.repo is not None \\\n                and exists(self.path):  # gc might be late, so the (temporary)\n                                        # repo doesn't exist on FS anymore\n\n            self.repo.git.clear_cache()\n            if exists(opj(self.path, '.git')):  # don't try to write otherwise\n                self.repo.index.write()\n````\n\nThis seem to indicate that without our explicit intervention GitPython would leave a repo in some kind of \"broken\" state. I somehow find that odd and unlikely.\n\n`git blame` says that some parts of this code where added in https://github.com/datalad/datalad/commit/1aeacf711306743ef493b56928cee5062e6df831 by @bpoldrack as a \"proper cleanup\". I assume, it is related to our reuse approach for Repo instances, but I cannot quite grasp how exactly.\n\nInterestingly, this also included running `precommit()`, which since then has been identified as a source of evil and was removed.\n\nFrom the commit message it is unclear to me how important this is. In #1635 we can already see that the conditionals in this code are not sufficient. Please let me know what you think about this. I am inclined to simply remove this code.","files":null}]}